es). So total non-sunny lines = v + w = n - k + v? Wait, total non-sunny lines = n - k. Among them, v are vertical, w = n - k - v are horizontal or slope -1. So w = n - k - v.\n\nNow, the capacity of non-vertical lines (including sunny lines) to cover points in a column is: each sunny line can cover at most 1 point per column, each horizontal or slope -1 line can also cover at most 1 point per column. So total lines that can intersect a column (excluding vertical lines) is k + w = k + (n - k - v) = n - v. So each column without a vertical line can be intersected by at most n - v lines (the total number of non-vertical lines). So the maximum number of points that can be covered in a column without a vertical line is n - v (since each line can intersect at most one point). So we need h_c <= n - v for each column c not vertical. So we need to choose v such that for all columns not vertical, h_c <= n - v. Since h_c decreases from n to 1, the worst case is the largest column not vertical. So we need to ensure that the largest column not vertical has height <= n - v. That is, if we don't put a vertical line on column 1 (height n), we need n <= n - v => v <= 0, which is impossible unless v=0. So we must put a vertical line on column 1. Similarly, if we don't put a vertical line on column 2 (height n-1), we need n-1 <= n - v => v <= 1. So we can have at most one column among the top two without a vertical line. In general, we need to choose v such that for each column not vertical, its height <= n - v. Since heights are decreasing, the condition is that the (v+1)-th largest height (i.e., column v+1) must satisfy h_{v+1} <= n - v. Since h_{c} = n+1 - c, we have h_{v+1} = n+1 - (v+1) = n - v. So equality holds. So we can have exactly v columns with vertical lines (the v leftmost columns), and the remaining columns (c > v) have heights h_c = n+1 - c <= n - v (since c >= v+1). Indeed, for c = v+1, h = n - v; for larger c, h smaller. So the condition is satisfied if we put vertical lines on the v leftmost columns. So we can choose any v from 0 to n, and then the remaining columns can be covered by non-vertical lines (including sunny lines) as long as we have enough lines to intersect each column at most once. But we also need to ensure that the total number of non-vertical lines (including sunny) is at least the number of points in each column? Actually each column c not vertical has h_c points, and we have n - v lines that can intersect it (the total number of non-vertical lines). Since each line can intersect at most one point in that column, we need n - v >= h_c for each column c not vertical. As we saw, this holds if we choose v such that v >= 0? Let's check: For column c = v+1, h_c = n - v, and n - v = number of non-vertical lines, so equality holds. So we can cover column v+1's points using each non-vertical line intersecting a distinct point in that column. For column v+2, h = n - v - 1, which is less than n - v, so we have enough lines to cover them. So the capacity condition is satisfied.\n\nThus, in principle, we could cover all points using v vertical lines (covering leftmost v columns) and the remaining n - v lines (including sunny and possibly horizontal/slope -1) each intersect each remaining column at exactly one point, covering all points. This is reminiscent of a Latin rectangle or a permutation matrix: we need to assign each of the n - v non-vertical lines to a distinct point in each column, such that each line picks exactly one point per column, and each column's points are all assigned to distinct lines. This is essentially a problem of constructing a set of n - v lines that intersect each column at exactly one point, covering all points in those columns. This is equivalent to constructing a set of n - v permutations (or matchings) that cover the points of a triangular grid. This is reminiscent of constructing a set of lines with distinct slopes that pass through points (c, something). But we also have constraints on sl