(5,4) (y=4,sum=9). L4 hasn't gotten any point from column 7 yet. It could get (7,1) but we assigned that to L2. So L4 remains with only (5,4). That's okay.\n\nNow column 8 point (8,1) (y=1,sum=9). We need to assign it to a line that does not have y=1 or sum=9 already.\n\nCurrent line states:\n\n- L1: y's {1,2}, sums {6,8}\n- L2: y's {2,3,1}, sums {7,9,8}\n- L3: y's {3,1,2}, sums {8,7,9}\n- L4: y's {4}, sums {9}\n\nCheck each line:\n\n- L1: y=1 duplicate, sum=9 not present (L1 sums {6,8}). Duplicate y, cannot.\n\n- L2: y=1 duplicate (L2 has y=1 from (7,1)), sum=9 duplicate (L2 has sum=9 from (6,3)). So cannot.\n\n- L3: y=1 duplicate (L3 has y=1 from (6,1) actually L3 has (6,1) y=1), sum=9 duplicate (L3 has sum=9 from (7,2)). So cannot.\n\n- L4: y=4 distinct, sum=9 duplicate (L4 sum=9 from (5,4)). Duplicate sum, cannot.\n\nThus, no line can take (8,1) without violating distinct y or sum constraints. Therefore, this assignment fails.\n\nThus, we need to adjust earlier assignments to allow (8,1) to be placed. The issue is that sum=9 appears in many lines: (5,4) sum=9, (6,3) sum=9, (7,2) sum=9, (8,1) sum=9. Since we have only 4 lines, each line can have at most one point with sum=9. So we need to distribute the four points with sum=9 across the four lines, each line gets exactly one. The points with sum=9 are: (5,4), (6,3), (7,2), (8,1). So each line must contain exactly one of these points. Similarly, points with sum=8 are: (5,3), (6,2), (7,1). There are 3 points with sum=8, they must be assigned to 3 distinct lines (cannot share a line). Points with sum=7: (5,2), (6,1). Points with sum=6: (5,1). So we need to assign points such that each line gets at most one point from each sum group.\n\nSimilarly, for y-values: y=1 appears in points (5,1),(6,1),(7,1),(8,1). So each line can have at most one y=1 point. So we need to assign each of these four points to distinct lines. Similarly, y=2 appears in (5,2),(6,2),(7,2). So at most one per line. y=3 appears in (5,3),(6,3). y=4 