ints are triples (a,b,s) with s = a+b. So each point belongs to exactly one column, one row, one diagonal. The condition that each line (color) can have at most one point per column, row, diagonal is exactly that each color class forms a partial transversal of the 3-dimensional Latin cube. This is reminiscent of a 3-dimensional Latin square of order n: we need to partition the set of triples (i,j,k) with i+j=k (mod something) into n partial transversals. This is similar to the problem of decomposing the triangular grid into n lines of slopes not 0,∞,-1. The solution's claim that only k=0,1,3 are possible suggests that the maximum number of sunny lines is 3. This is reminiscent of a known result: the triangular grid cannot be covered by n lines with more than 3 non-axis-aligned lines. But we need to verify.\n\nWe can try to see if a 4-coloring exists for n=8. If we can find a 4-coloring satisfying the constraints, then we can construct 4 sunny lines (one per color) covering all points, with the remaining n-4 lines being vertical (or other non-sunny). However, we also need to ensure that the lines are distinct. The coloring approach would produce lines that are not necessarily distinct? Actually each color class would correspond to a set of points that are collinear with a sunny slope. But the coloring condition ensures that each color class has at most one point per column, row, diagonal, but does not guarantee collinearity. The collinearity condition is stronger: the points of a color class must lie on a single line. The coloring condition is necessary but not sufficient for collinearity. However, if we can find a coloring where each color class is a line (i.e., the points are collinear), then we have a solution. The coloring approach is a way to think about the constraints.\n\nThus, the existence of a 4-coloring with each color class being a line is a stronger condition. The solution's claim that k≥4 is impossible suggests that such a configuration cannot exist.\n\