\n\nNow assign column 4 points (3 points) to three of the lines, ensuring no y or sum conflict.\n\nColumn 4 points: (4,1) (y=1,sum=5), (4,2) (y=2,sum=6), (4,3) (y=3,sum=7).\n\nCheck each line:\n\n- L1 currently has y=1,sum=4. So cannot take (4,1) (y=1 duplicate) or (4,2) (sum=6 distinct, y=2 distinct) or (4,3) (sum=7 distinct, y=3 distinct). So L1 can take (4,2) or (4,3). Let's assign (4,2) to L1 (y=2 distinct, sum=6 distinct). So L1 now has (3,1) and (4,2).\n\n- L2 currently has y=2,sum=5. Cannot take (4,2) (y=2 duplicate) or (4,1) (sum=5 duplicate) or (4,3) (y=3 distinct, sum=7 distinct). So L2 can take (4,3). Assign (4,3) to L2. L2 now has (3,2) and (4,3).\n\n- L3 currently has y=3,sum=6. Cannot take (4,3) (y=3 duplicate) or (4,2) (sum=6 duplicate) or (4,1) (y=1 distinct, sum=5 distinct). So L3 can take (4,1). Assign (4,1) to L3. L3 now has (3,3) and (4,1).\n\n- L4 currently has y=4,sum=7. It cannot take any column 4 point because all column 4 points have sum 5,6,7. L4 sum=7, so cannot take (4,3) (sum=7 duplicate). It could take (4,1) sum=5 distinct, y=1 distinct, but (4,1) already assigned to L3. It could take (4,2) sum=6 distinct, y=2 distinct, but (4,2) assigned to L1. So L4 will not get a point from column 4. That's okay; L4 will have only (3,4) from column 3.\n\nNow column 5 points: (5,1) (y=1,sum=6), (5,2) (y=2,sum=7). We need to assign each to a distinct line (since each line can have at most one point per column). We have 4 lines, but only 2 points, so we can assign them to any two lines that can accept them.\n\nCheck each line's current y and sum sets:\n\n- L1: y's {1,2}, sums {4,6}. So L1 cannot take (5,1) (y=1 duplicate) or (5,2) (y=2 duplicate) or sum duplicates? (5,1) sum=6 duplicate (L1 sum includes 6 from (4,2)). So L1 cannot take either point.\n\n- L2: y's {2,3}, sums {5,7}. L2 cannot take (5,1) (y=1 distinct, sum=6 distinct) => allowed. L2 cannot take (5,2) (y=2 duplicate). So L2 can take (5,1). Let's assign (5,1) to L2. L2 now has (3,2),(4,3),(5,1).\n\n- L3: y's {3,1}, sums {6,5}. L3 cannot take (5,1) (y=1 duplicate) or (5,2) (y=2 distinct, sum=7 distinct) => allowed. So assign (5,2) to L3. L3 now has (3,3),(4,1),(5,2).\n\n- L4: y's {4}, sum {7}. L4 cannot take (5,1) (sum=6 distinct, y=1 distinct) => allowed. L4 cannot take (5,2) (sum=7 duplicate). So L4 can take (5,1). But (5,1) already assigned to L2. We need each point assigned to exactly one line. So we need to assign (5,1) to L2, (5,2) to L3, and L4 remains without a point from column 5. That's okay.\n\nNow column 6 point: (6,1) (y=1,sum=7). We need to assign it to a line that can accept it.\n\nCheck each line:\n\n- L1: y's {1,2}, sum {4,6}. Cannot take (6,1) (y=1 duplicate) or sum=7 distinct but y duplicate, so no.\n\n- L2: y's {2,3,1}, sum {5,7,6? Actually L2 sums: (3,2) sum=5, (4,3) sum=7, (5,1) sum=6}. So L2 has y's {2,3,1}, sums {5,7,6}. (6,1) y=1 duplicate, sum=7 duplicate (both duplicate). So cannot.\n\n- L3: y's {3,1,2}, sums {6,5,7? Actually L3: (3,3) sum=6, (4,1) sum=5, (5,2) sum=7}. So L3 has y's {3,1,2}, sums {6,5,7}. (6,1) y=1 duplicate, sum=7 duplicate. So cannot.\n\n- L4: y's {4}, sum {7}. (6,1) y=1 distinct, sum=7 duplicate (L4 sum=7). So cannot.\n\nThus, no line can take (6,1). So this assignment fails.\n\nWe need to adjust earlier assignments to allow a line to take (6,1). Since (6,1) has y=1, sum=7, we need a line that does not already have y=1 or sum=7. Let's see which lines have sum=7 currently: L2 has sum=7 (from (4,3)), L3 has sum=7 (from (5,2)), L4 has sum=7 (from initial (3,4) sum=7). So all lines except L1 have sum=7. L1 sum set is {4,6}. So L1 does not have sum=7, but L1 has y=1 (from (3,1)). So L1 cannot take (6,1) because y duplicate. So we need a line that has neither y=1 nor sum=7. Currently, all lines have either y=1 or sum=7. So we need to adjust assignments to free up a line.\n\nWe could try to assign (3,4) (which gave L4 sum=7) to a different line, maybe L1, to free L4 from sum=7. But L1 already has sum=6 and 4, and y=1,2. L1 cannot take (3,4) because sum=7 distinct, y=4 distinct, but L1 already has y=1,2, sum=4,6. So L1 could take (3,4) as a third point (since each line can have multiple points across columns). However, L1 currently has points (3,1) and (4,2). Adding (3,4) would be a second point in column 3, which is not allowed (each line can intersect a column at most one point). So we cannot assign (3,4) to L1 because L1 already has a point in column 3 (3,1). So we need to reassign column 3 points to different lines.\n\nThus, we need to find a different assignment of column 3 points to lines such that we can free up a line without sum=7 and y=1. Let's try a different initial assignment for column 3 points.\n\nWe have 4 lines, need to assign (3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4) to them. Let's try to assign them such that the line that will later take (6,1) does not have y=1 or sum=7. Since (6,1) has y=1, sum=7, we need a line with y ≠ 1 and sum ≠ 7. So we need to assign a column 3 point to that line that does not have y=1 or sum=7. The column 3 points have:\n\n- (3,1): y=1, sum=4\n- (3,2): y=2, sum=5\n- (3,3): y=3, sum=6\n- (3,4): y=4, sum=7\n\nThus, the only column 3 point with sum=7 is (3,4). So if we assign (3,4) to the line that will later take (6,1), that line will have sum=7 already, which is not allowed. So we must assign (3,4) to a different line, not the one that will take (6,1). So the line that will take (6,1) must be assigned a column 3 point with sum ≠ 7 and y ≠ 1. Options: (3,2) (y=2,sum=5) or (3,3) (y=3,sum=6). Both have sum not 7 and y not 1. So we can assign (3,2) or (3,3) to the line that will later take (6,1). Let's call that line Lx.\n\nNow, we also need to assign column 4 points (4,1) sum=5,y=1; (4,2) sum=6,y=2; (4,3) sum=7,y=3. The line Lx cannot have y=1 or sum=7. So it cannot take (4,1) (y=1) or (4,3) (sum=7). It could take (4,2) (y=2,sum=6) if those are not already used in Lx. If Lx has (3,2) (y=2,sum=5), then (4,2) has y=2 duplicate, not allowed. If Lx has (3,3) (y=3,sum=6), then (4,2) sum=6 duplicate, not allowed. So Lx cannot take any column 4 point if it has (3,2) or (3,3). So Lx will not intersect column 4 at a required point, which is okay; it can intersect column 4 at a non-required point.\n\nNow column 5 points: (5,1) sum=6,y=1; (5,2) sum=7,y=2. Lx cannot have y=1 or sum=7. So it cannot take (5,1) (y=1) nor (5,2) (sum=7). So Lx cannot intersect column 5 at a required point either. That's okay; it can intersect column 5 at a non-required point.\n\nThus, Lx will only intersect columns 3 and 6 (maybe also column 2) at required points. It will intersect column 6 at (6,1). It will intersect column 3 at either (3,2) or (3,3). It may also intersect column 2 at some point (since we have 2 non-sunny lines covering columns 1 and 2). Actually we have vertical lines covering columns 1 and 2, so column 2 is already covered by a vertical line, so Lx does not need to intersect column 2 at a required point. So Lx can have only two required points: (3,2) or (3,3) and (6,1). That's okay.\n\nThus, we can try to construct a configuration with vertical lines at columns 1 and 2, and 4 sunny lines covering columns 3-6, with one of the sunny lines covering only (6,1) and maybe (3,2) or (3,3). The other three sunny lines must cover the remaining points in columns 3-5.\n\nLet's attempt to construct such a configuration.\n\nWe have vertical lines: x=1 (covers column 1 points (1,1)-(1,6)), x=2 (covers column 2 points (2,1)-(2,5)). Now we need to cover columns 3-6 with 4 sunny lines.\n\nPoints in columns 3-6:\n\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4)\nColumn 4: (4,1),(4,2),(4,3)\nColumn 5: (5,1),(5,2)\nColumn 6: (6,1)\n\nTotal points: 4+3+2+1 = 10 points.\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines. Let's denote them S1,S2,S3,S4.\n\nWe need to assign points to these lines such that each line gets at most one point per column, and each line's points are collinear with a sunny slope.\n\nWe can try to assign S1 to cover (6,1) and (3,2) (or (3,3)). Let's try S1: points (