ersect column 5 at (5,1) or (5,2) and also intersect column 3 and column 4 appropriately.\n\nLet's try to find a line that passes through (5,1) and a point in column 3, and also a point in column 4 (maybe). For each column 3 point (3,i), we can compute the line through (5,1) and (3,i) and see if it intersects column 4 at an integer y within allowed range.\n\nLine through (5,1) and (3,i): slope = (i-1)/(3-5) = (i-1)/(-2) = (1-i)/2. At x=4, y = 1 + slope*(4-5) = 1 + (1-i)/2 * (-1) = 1 - (1-i)/2 = (2 - (1-i))/2 = (1 + i)/2. So y at column 4 is (1+i)/2. For this to be integer, i must be odd. i can be 1,3. For i=1, y = (1+1)/2 = 1. So line through (5,1) and (3,1) is horizontal (slope 0), not sunny. So not allowed. For i=3, y = (1+3)/2 = 2. So line through (5,1) and (3,3) has slope (3-1)/(-2) = -1, not sunny. So not allowed. So no sunny line through (5,1) and a column 3 point.\n\nNow line through (5,2) and (3,i): slope = (i-2)/(3-5) = (i-2)/(-2) = (2-i)/2. At x=4, y = 2 + slope*(4-5) = 2 + (2-i)/2 * (-1) = 2 - (2-i)/2 = (4 - (2-i))/2 = (2 + i)/2. So y = (i+2)/2. For integer, i must be even. i can be 2,4. For i=2, y = (2+2)/2 = 2. So line through (5,2) and (3,2) has slope (2-2)/(-2) = 0, horizontal, not sunny. For i=4, y = (4+2)/2 = 3. So line through (5,2) and (3,4) has slope (4-2)/(-2) = -1, not sunny. So no sunny line through (5,2) and a column 3 point.\n\nThus, no sunny line can intersect column 5 at a required point and also intersect column 3 at a required point. So any line covering a column 5 point cannot also cover a column 3 point. It could cover column 4 maybe? Let's check line through (5,1) and (4,i). For (5,1) and (4,i): slope = (i-1)/(4-5) = (i-1)/(-1) = 1-i. At x=3, y = 1 + (1-i)*(3-5) = 1 + (1-i)*(-2) = 1 -2(1-i) = 1 -2 +2i = 2i -1. For i=1, y = 1, line is horizontal (slope 0). For i=2, y = 3, which is not a required point in column 3 (since column 3 has y up to 4). Actually column 3 has y=1..4, so y=3 is allowed. So line through (5,1) and (4,2) has slope =